### Where the time goes

These are the largest differences we measured, and each is a target for future work in go2cs.

- **Start-up.** A converted program spends about [[JIT_START]] s before it does any work: compiling methods on
  first call, running every package's initializers, and building Go-style method sets by reflection the first time a
  type is asserted to an interface. Native AOT removes the compiling; the rest remains. Native Go starts in
  milliseconds.
- **Garbage collection.** The converted compiler allocates much more than the Go original, and collection pauses
  were about [[GC_SHARE]] of the type-check time. The demo projects here turn on server garbage collection with
  dynamic adaptation off, which cut [[SERVER_GC_GAIN]] in our measurements. go2cs's
  [tuning guide](https://github.com/ritchiecarroll/go2cs/blob/master/docs/Tuning.md) explains the setting.
- **Field views on embedded structs.** Each AST node reaches its embedded base through a cached field view, and those
  views were about [[VIEW_SHARE]] of all bytes allocated.
- **Struct hash keys.** Maps keyed by structs hash through .NET's general value-type hash, where Go hashes the
  fields directly.
- **Range-over-func iterators** run on coroutines, which cost a thread hand-off where Go makes a direct call.

Fixed in go2cs since the first measurement: a string conversion that copied the rest of the source text for each
non-ASCII character (the largest single source of allocation), nil checks on named maps that went through
reflection, and struct equality that boxed both operands.

## What is not here

The language service, the language server, `tsc`'s command line and watch mode are not converted here. Five of
TypeScript's packages (`internal/ls`, `internal/ls/lsconv`, `internal/ls/autoimport`, `internal/project` and
`internal/fourslash`) use a generic-constraint shape go2cs does not convert yet, and everything that imports them is
left out with them.

